                                    "topic_metadata": {
                                        "topic_id": "MAT-LS-S4-T1-0003",
                                        "topic_title": "Linear Programming",
                                        "sequence_order": 3,
                                        "recommended_duration_periods": 20
                                    },
                                    "core_competency": "The learner understands and uses linear programming to solve problems.",
                                    "prerequisites": [],
                                    "learning_outcomes": [
                                        {
                                            "outcome_text": "form linear inequalities based on real life situations. (u, s)",
                                            "domain": "u, s"
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "outcome_text": "represent the inequalities on the graph and identifies the required region. (u, s)",
                                            "domain": "u, s"
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "outcome_text": "find and interpret the optimum solution of a set of linear inequalities in two unknowns. (u, s, v/a)",
                                            "domain": "u, s, v/a"
                                        }
                                    ],
                                    "content_scope": {
                                        "must_cover": [],
                                        "optional_enrichment": [],
                                        "explicitly_excluded": [],
                                        "depth_limitations": []
                                    },
                                    "suggested_learning_activities": [
                                        {
                                            "activity_type": "Problem Solving/Discussion",
                                            "description": "Peter has UGX 900 000. He wants to buy some apples and oranges. Apples cost UGX 10 000 each and oranges cost UGX 600 each. He wants to buy at least 22 apples and at least 10 oranges. The number of apples bought must be more than twice the number of oranges bought.\nIn groups, discuss how to form the inequalities that must be satisfied. Plot them on a coordinate grid and propose a solution."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "activity_type": "Problem Solving",
                                            "description": "Use linear programming to solve this problem: A small business makes 3-speed and 10-speed bicycles.\no Factory A produces 16 3-speed and 20 10-speed bicycles each day. It costs UGX 100M each day to operate factory A.\no Factory B produces 12 3-speed and 20 10-speed bicycles each day. It costs UGX 80M each day to operate factory B.\nAn order for 96 3-speed bicycles and 140 10-speed bicycles has just arrived. How many days should each factory operate in order to fill the order at minimum cost.\nWhat is the minimum cost?"
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "activity_type": "Creation/Pair Work",
                                            "description": "In pairs, make up a linear programming problem for your peers."
                                        }
                                    ],
                                    "suggested_assessment_activities": [
                                        {
                                            "assessment_type": "Observation/Task",
                                            "description": "In pairs, let the learners discuss the following task. Observe them as they discuss. Assess the learners on the core values and generic skills.\n1. A mother buys x notes books at UGX 600 each and y pens for UGX 800 each. She has UGX 8000 to spend and there must be at least 4 note books and at least 4 pens.\nWrite three inequalities in x and y that satisfy these conditions.\nIllustrate them graphically by shading out the unwanted regions. Write down the integer solution set.\n2. A farmer has 32 acres on which to plant corn and soybean.\nFor each acre of corn planted, the expenses are UGX 100 000, and for each acre of soybean planted it is UGX 200 000.\nEach acre of corn requires 100 bushels of storage and yields a profit of UGX 120 000.\nEach acre of soybean requires 40 bushels of storage and yields a profit of UGX 180 000.\nIf the total amount of storage space is 1920 bushels and the farmer has only UGX 8M to pay for planting, how many acres of each crop should be planted to maximize the profit?\nWhat will the profit be?"
                                        }
                                    ],
